<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sook Ching</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sook Ching massacre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sook Ching, meaning &quot;purge through cleansing&quot;, was the mass killing of ethnic Chinese by the Japanese military in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> in February 1942. Chinese men were screened at registration centres, and those suspected of anti-Japanese sympathies were taken away and shot. Estimates of the dead range from about 25,000 to 50,000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sook-ching.jpg" alt="Sook Ching" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deoma12 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Civilian_War_Memorial_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
